I certainly have learned to first ask  who the director is obviously the script  and the character is important but  filmmaking is a director's medium in my  point of view and you really need to be  able to trust that director work with  that director and know that that  director is going to push to get the  best out of you I love to do a scene 20  different ways but I can only play and  go off in this tangent that tangent if  I'm really conferring the director knows  exactly what he wants from the scene and  knows that he's gonna get that and you  might get some surprises if you go left  and right but if that makes sense the  directors that I most like working with  give you a freedom within these  boundaries it's kind of like parenting  though because we are kids after all  and then when I'm in rehearsal I like to  collaborate I like to talk I like to  rehearse some directors don't like to  rehearse and I respect that  but I'm open I talk about my process to  them I talk about how I'm feeling I talk  about questions I have even ones that  might a feet up here or I'm worried or  stupid questions I'll always get them  out because I think the last thing you  ever want to have in front of the camera  is doubt in any way shape or form about  the character you're playing about what  you story you're trying to tell about  what you're trying to achieve I  genuinely like to be friendly with  everyone in the Australian film industry  everyone talks to everyone and if  they're not making fun of you then they  really don't like you so there's a  problem and I also when I first went to  America I noticed there was an unspoken  it's not people tell stories have don't  look this actor in the eye and all that  I've never heard that before but there  is a slightly unwritten rule of leave  the actor alone let them do their thing  it could be our process it could be  because their style not sure and I  always try literally on the first day to  break that down because I feel  self-conscious in that environment I'd  much prefer a grip you know making fun  of me then calling me mr. Jackman and  then not saying nothing until they say  thank you for there's been great working  with you you know I much  prefer to feel we're in it together when  I first began to get over my nerves or  auditioning I used to always go into an  audition as though it was a rehearsal  more like the first rehearsal so I would  always ask the director or casting agent  whoever was questions if they said look  I think you should sit sit down the  chair say you know I think the character  would stand it was just my lip way of  trying to redress the balance and so I  wasn't just you've got all the power and  I'm trying to please you but rather  we're trying to work together so that  was how I and that seemed to work I  often get that advice to young actors I  certainly think Wolverine was a risk for  me when I first got it thankfully nobody  really knew who I was I mean in the  comic book he was five foot five and not  really the kind of personality that I am  so no one really knew that so I got to  thankfully just go in an audition but  only I had never heard of x-men when I  got the job and I started telling people  and when I was shooting it as well only  when I started to realize the immense  passion that the fans have and how  important that role was and potentially  what was at stake for me I reveled in  the physical side of Wolverine I took it  very seriously I spent a long time  studying animals and trying to work out  ways to incorporate what had been an  image on a page for 40 years and how to  make that work the art in the comic  books itself was really informative I  used to kind of some of those moves in  there and those artists their economy  that how they can tell so much of an  entire fight and maybe eight frames it  was extraordinary so I really drew on  them and the movement to me has always  been very important I will spend  sometimes hours walking around a room  inhabiting a character and I might just  start from the top down or from the feet  from the toes right through to the top  my head imagining how their character  walks how that character sits how their  character holds himself when they're  angry how they sleep all that stuff  because  it really informs how you play the  character I remember a McKellen saying  to me just because it feels  uncomfortable doesn't mean it's not good  and I remember I am I doing the first  x-men movie was a little I came in late  I felt a bit behind the 8-ball I started  shooting four weeks into filming because  I was replacing the guy who was playing  the role so I came in feeling a bit  behind the eight ball and there was a  rhythm going and I I just felt a bit  uncomfortable and and I I remember him  giving me that advice he goes your stuff  is good and on even if it feels  uncomfortable that can sometimes be  right I think so often not only on film  but in life we so want to feel  comfortable but that doesn't always  bring the best results if I look back I  think some of my more successful things  and my point of view have been the  things I've been most nervous about and  probably most unsure when I began  there's something about jumping off that  cliff which means you end up going for  broke and probably end up taking a few  more risks if you know what I mean and I  would say that young actors it's okay to  be nervous  say you nervous I used to by the way for  20 years I never did I hit it  tried to hide it say it and it's usually  relieving to everyone around you maybe  not on the 20th take but certainly when  you walk in it's fine  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

